    "description": "Iranian cultural life encompasses shared celebrations, regional skills, social conventions, and traditions transmitted through families and teachers. These practices overlap across linguistic communities and national borders, while their meanings depend on the setting and the people involved.",
    "tags": ["Iranian culture", "Living heritage", "Social customs", "Regional traditions", "Cultural transmission", "Celebrations"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:16:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:16:22Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "People, Values & Culture in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Iranian celebrations, courtesy, music, craft, and family transmission while distinguishing shared traditions from regional and individual variation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "culture-as-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Begin with what people do",
        "content": "A useful introduction to Iranian culture begins with practices: visiting relatives for the spring new year, learning a musical repertoire from a teacher, choosing colours for a woven carpet, or negotiating courtesy at a doorway. Each involves knowledge that people acquire through participation. Together they reveal culture as something performed and transmitted, rather than simply a collection of monuments or famous names. [s1][s2][s3][s4]\n\nThese examples should not become a personality profile for an entire population. A description of a celebration establishes its documented forms; it does not demonstrate that every household observes it. Likewise, the practices recorded in a particular weaving community cannot stand for all Iranian crafts. Reading the evidence at its proper scale makes room for both shared references and local difference. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "identity-and-language",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Belonging has several layers",
        "content": "Iranian identity can coexist with attachment to a locality, language, religious community, or family network. Historical scholarship describes these affiliations as interacting, sometimes competing forms of belonging. A national label therefore does not exhaust how someone understands their place in society. [s5]\n\nLanguage requires similar care. In linguistics, Iranian names a family of related languages, rather than every language used by citizens of Iran. Writing systems also cross linguistic boundaries: scholarly documentation includes Iranian languages written in Arabic-derived, Hebrew, Cyrillic, and other scripts. Language, script, nationality, and cultural affiliation answer different questions. Keeping them distinct helps readers approach the Languages & Dialects branch without treating Persian as the entirety of Iranian linguistic life. [s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s5", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "seasonal-celebrations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Seasonal gatherings connect food, memory, and relationships",
        "content": "Nowruz, meaning new day, marks spring's arrival. UNESCO describes visits to relatives and friends, shared meals, new clothes, and gifts among its practices. Its geography extends well beyond Iran, making it an example of cultural connection across borders. Shared participation does not require identical observance everywhere. [s1]\n\nYalda, also called Chella, provides another seasonal setting. UNESCO's account describes gatherings on autumn's final night in Iran and Afghanistan, often at elders' homes, with fruits, nuts, poetry, stories, games, and music. Food here belongs to a social occasion: what matters is also who gathers, what is recited, and how younger participants encounter the custom. These celebrations connect the Cultural Celebrations, Food & Cooking, and Family & Lifestyle branches. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "courtesy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Courtesy is an interaction",
        "content": "Taarof is a complex of ritual courtesy involving speech and gestures. Anthropologist William O. Beeman describes language that elevates the addressee or lowers the speaker, alongside actions such as yielding passage or offering an honoured seat. Offers and refusals can express respect, generosity, social position, or strategic pressure. [s4]\n\nThis complexity matters to international readers. Translating taarof as mere insincerity loses the possibility of genuine goodwill; translating it simply as kindness misses its relationship to hierarchy and obligation. It is more useful to notice the participants and circumstances than to apply a mechanical rule about how often an offer should be refused. The Social Customs branch can examine these interactions in their particular settings. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "craft-and-performance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Craft and performance depend on skilled transmission",
        "content": "UNESCO's description of carpet weaving in Fars, in southwestern Iran, follows work from wool preparation and loom construction through spinning, dyeing, and weaving. It emphasizes women's design decisions and learning through demonstration. A carpet can therefore be approached as the outcome of material knowledge and labour, as well as a visual object. The account concerns the nominated tradition; its division of tasks should not be assumed for every workshop or household. [s3]\n\nThe radif is a repertoire underlying Iranian classical music. Musicians learn melodic material through sustained instruction, then draw on it in performance and improvisation. This relationship between memory and invention challenges the idea that preserving a tradition means reproducing an object without variation. Oral Traditions & Folklore and Clothing & Everyday Crafts offer complementary routes into how knowledge passes between people. [s7]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s7"]
      },
      {
        "id": "continuity-and-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Living heritage changes its channels",
        "content": "Transmission is not confined to face-to-face teaching. UNESCO's Yalda account identifies broadcasting, publications, social media, and educational materials alongside family learning. These channels help explain why a practice can retain familiar references while reaching participants through different settings. They do not, by themselves, measure its prevalence or show that everyone's experience has changed in the same way. [s2]\n\nThe remaining branches invite questions that heritage descriptions alone cannot answer: how apartment life affects visiting, how young people negotiate inherited expectations, or how work and consumption shape everyday relationships. This introduction supplies documented cultural examples, while fuller coverage of contemporary lifestyles requires additional social research. Readers should approach each branch by asking whose practice is described, where it occurs, and what evidence supports claims about its reach. [s2][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s5"]
      }
    ],
