  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-2-1",
    "page_path": "/explore/culture/iranian-identity/",
    "name": "Iranian Identity",
    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/culture/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iranian identity draws on changing relationships among territory, historical memory, language, religion, and local belonging. Its long cultural history needs to be distinguished from the institutions and political claims of modern nationalism.",
    "tags": ["Iranian identity", "Historical memory", "National identity", "Local identities", "Cultural history"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:16:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:16:22Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Identity: History, Memory and Belonging | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian identity through ancient political language, medieval cultural memory, modern nationalism, and overlapping local affiliations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "definitions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Several meanings of belonging",
        "content": "Iranian identity can refer to identification with a country, participation in a cultural inheritance, or attachment to a historical idea of Iran. These meanings overlap, but they should not be treated as interchangeable. A modern citizen, a medieval author, and an ancient ruler occupied different political and cultural worlds. [s1][s2]\n\nHistorical scholarship also describes national belonging alongside kinship, language, locality, and religious affiliation. Such attachments can reinforce one another or become sources of tension. This makes identity a subject of history and interpretation rather than a checklist of traits that every Iranian must possess. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ancient-language",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Ancient references need historical boundaries",
        "content": "Gherardo Gnoli distinguishes early cultural and ethnolinguistic references from the political idea articulated under the Sasanians. His account places a clearly expressed political conception of Iran in the early third century CE, associated with Ardashir I. This distinction prevents the modern country from being projected unchanged into every earlier empire. [s3]\n\nSasanian royal language connected political authority to a conception of Iran and its relationship to lands beyond it. Such evidence establishes how rulers represented their realm; it does not reveal a uniform personal identity among all inhabitants. Nor does an ancient designation provide a map of contemporary borders. The historical question concerns how names, institutions, and cultural narratives acquired meaning in a particular setting. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "medieval-reconstruction",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cultural memory survived through reconstruction",
        "content": "After the Arab conquest and the end of Sasanian government, Iranian cultural identification developed within Islamic societies. Ahmad Ashraf emphasizes the role of literati, the emergence of literary New Persian, and renewed engagement with inherited history. Continuity involved selection and reinterpretation, rather than the unchanged survival of a previous political order. [s4]\n\nThe Shahnameh, Ferdowsi's epic account of kings and heroes, became an important vehicle for this historical imagination. Its narratives bring together legend and remembered history; they should not be read as a modern documentary chronology. Poetry, storytelling, and references to the lands of Iran helped sustain a cultural vocabulary across changing dynasties. [s4]\n\nThis history also complicates ancestry-based definitions. Persianized Turkic and Mongol rulers could place themselves within Iranian traditions of kingship. Participation in a political and literary culture therefore cannot be reduced to a single lineage. The wider reach of Persian culture also means that its history exceeds the borders of Iran today. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "modern-interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Modern nationalism reorganized the past",
        "content": "Modern nationalism changed the framework in which older memories were interpreted. Ashraf's survey distinguishes approaches that treat nations as ancient and enduring, approaches that emphasize their modern construction, and historical approaches that investigate connections without assuming an unchanged national essence. These are competing analytical positions, not interchangeable descriptions. [s2]\n\nThe nineteenth and twentieth centuries brought new uses of history in national argument. Ashraf describes nationalist writing that increasingly emphasized the Achaemenid past, alongside interpretations that located Iranian identity in intertwined Iranian and Islamic histories. The disagreement concerns which inheritances should define the nation and how they should relate to political authority. [s5]\n\nA careful account can recognize the antiquity of names and memories while still asking how modern writers and institutions rearranged them. Older cultural material does not make every later nationalist claim historically inevitable. [s2][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-branches",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Names, symbols, and local identities answer different questions",
        "content": "The branches below separate several kinds of evidence. The Idea of Iranian Identity examines concepts and interpretations. The Name Iran through History concerns historical usage. National Symbols and Historical Flags of Iran concern objects and representations whose meanings depend on their date and users. National & Local Identities examines overlapping forms of belonging. This organization helps readers avoid treating a symbol, a language, and a legal nationality as equivalent evidence. [s1][s2]\n\nThe most useful question is often specific: who invoked Iran, in what setting, and for what purpose? That approach accommodates durable cultural attachments while leaving space for disagreement, regional experience, and historical change. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Iranian Identity",
        "publisher": "Encyclopaedia Iranica",
        "url": "https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/iranian-identity/",
        "language": "en",
        "publication_date": "2000-01-01",
        "access_date": "2026-10-27",
